{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21302","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21302","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21302","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chihao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Locus: Agentic Predicate Synthesis for Directed Fuzzing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T01:47:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:11:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T03:33:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-04T00:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:27:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-23T00:07:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3744916.3773102","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Directed fuzzing aims to find program inputs that lead to specified target program states. It has broad applications, such as debugging system crashes, confirming reported bugs, and generating exploits for potential vulnerabilities. This task is inherently challenging because target states are often deeply nested in the program, while the search space manifested by numerous possible program inputs is prohibitively large. Existing approaches rely on branch distances or manually-specified constraints to guide the search; however, the branches alone are often insufficient to precisely characterize progress toward reaching the target states, while the manually specified constraints are often tailored for specific bug types and thus difficult to generalize to diverse target states and programs.\n We present Locus, a novel framework to improve the efficiency of directed fuzzing. Our key insight is to synthesize predicates to capture fuzzing progress as semantically meaningful intermediate states, serving as milestones towards reaching the target states. When used to instrument the program under fuzzing, they can reject executions unlikely to reach the target states, while providing additional coverage guidance. To automate this task and generalize to diverse programs, Locus features an agentic framework with program analysis tools to synthesize and iteratively refine the candidate predicates, while ensuring the predicates strictly relax the target states to prevent false rejections via symbolic execution. Our evaluation shows that Locus substantially improves the efficiency of eight state-of-the-art fuzzers in discovering real-world vulnerabilities, achieving an average speedup of 41.6x. So far, Locus has found nine previously unpatched bugs, with three already acknowledged with draft patches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21302","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-01T01:23:51Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:23:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-23T02:07:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Jurayj","name":"Jurayj, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Holzenberger","name":"Holzenberger, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Van Durme","name":"Van Durme, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Models and Logic Programs for Trustworthy Tax Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T17:55:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T00:51:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-29T21:48:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:09:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T18:58:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T02:07:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"According to the United States Internal Revenue Service, ``the average American spends $\\$270$ and 13 hours filing their taxes''. Even beyond the U.S., tax filing requires complex reasoning, combining application of overlapping rules with numerical calculations. Because errors can incur costly penalties, any automated system must deliver high accuracy and auditability, making modern large language models (LLMs) poorly suited for this task. We propose an approach that integrates LLMs with a symbolic solver to calculate tax obligations. We evaluate variants of this system on the challenging StAtutory Reasoning Assessment (SARA) dataset, and include a novel method for estimating the cost of deploying such a system based on real-world penalties for tax errors. We further show how combining up-front translation of plain-text rules into formal logic programs, combined with intelligently retrieved exemplars for formal case representations, can dramatically improve performance on this task and reduce costs to well below real-world averages. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of applying semantic parsing methods to statutory reasoning, and show promising economic feasibility of neuro-symbolic architectures for increasing access to reliable tax assistance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AAAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-08-29T01:47:43Z","registered":"2025-08-29T01:47:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:20:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21422","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21422","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21422","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Dycke","name":"Dycke, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Gurevych","name":"Gurevych, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automatic Reviewers Fail to Detect Faulty Reasoning in Research Papers: A New Counterfactual Evaluation Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T08:48:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:22:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T10:29:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:42:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have great potential to accelerate and support scholarly peer review and are increasingly used as fully automatic review generators (ARGs). However, potential biases and systematic errors may pose significant risks to scientific integrity; understanding the specific capabilities and limitations of state-of-the-art ARGs is essential. We focus on a core reviewing skill that underpins high-quality peer review: detecting faulty research logic. This involves evaluating the internal consistency between a paper's results, interpretations, and claims. We present a fully automated counterfactual evaluation framework that isolates and tests this skill under controlled conditions. Testing a range of ARG approaches, we find that, contrary to expectation, flaws in research logic have no significant effect on their output reviews. Based on our findings, we derive three actionable recommendations for future work and release our counterfactual dataset and evaluation framework publicly."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to TACL 2026 (presented at EACL 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21422","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:26:43Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:26:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:23:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheldon","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Sheldon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junda","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xintong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritwik","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Ritwik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"McAuley","name":"McAuley, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explainable Chain-of-Thought Reasoning: An Empirical Analysis on State-Aware Reasoning Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T18:53:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:05:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T12:30:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:51:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting have enabled large language models (LLMs) to perform multi-step reasoning. However, the explainability of such reasoning remains limited, with prior work primarily focusing on local token-level attribution, such that the high-level semantic roles of reasoning steps and their transitions remain underexplored. In this paper, we introduce a state-aware transition framework that abstracts CoT trajectories into structured latent dynamics. Specifically, to capture the evolving semantics of CoT reasoning, each reasoning step is represented via spectral analysis of token-level embeddings and clustered into semantically coherent latent states. To characterize the global structure of reasoning, we model their progression as a Markov chain, yielding a structured and interpretable view of the reasoning process. This abstraction supports a range of analyses, including semantic role identification, temporal pattern visualization, and consistency evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:26:36Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:26:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:40:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushan","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Kushan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hannah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Estevam","familyName":"Hruschka","name":"Hruschka, Estevam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RECAP: REwriting Conversations for Intent Understanding in Agentic Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T20:45:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T08:45:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:30:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-24T00:09:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:11:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding user intent is essential for effective planning in conversational assistants, particularly those powered by large language models (LLMs) coordinating multiple agents. However, real-world dialogues are often ambiguous, underspecified, or dynamic, making intent detection a persistent challenge. Traditional classification-based approaches struggle to generalize in open-ended settings, leading to brittle interpretations and poor downstream planning. We propose RECAP (REwriting Conversations for Agent Planning), a new benchmark designed to evaluate and advance intent rewriting, reframing user-agent dialogues into concise representations of user goals. RECAP captures diverse challenges such as ambiguity, intent drift, vagueness, and mixed-goal conversations. Alongside the dataset, we introduce an LLM-based evaluator that assesses planning utility given the rewritten intent. Using RECAP, we develop a prompt-based rewriting approach that outperforms baselines, in terms of plan preference. We further demonstrate that fine-tuning two DPO-based rewriters yields additional utility gains. Our results highlight intent rewriting as a critical and tractable component for improving agentic planning in open-domain dialogue systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-08T01:25:47Z","registered":"2025-09-08T01:25:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:33:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rimom","familyName":"Costa","name":"Costa, Rimom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Instruction-Level Weight Shaping: A Framework for Self-Improving AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T21:34:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:09:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-20T18:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:34:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are fluent but largely static after pre-training; new or shifting knowledge is typically added with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) or fine-tuning. RAG raises latency and engineering overhead and often fails to integrate facts; prompt engineering is brittle and can conflict with prior knowledge; fine-tuning is costly and risks catastrophic forgetting. We propose Instruction-Level Weight Shaping (ILWS): curated system instructions act as external, auditable pseudo-parameters updated after each session via reflection and user feedback. A Reflection Engine inspects conversation traces, diagnoses reasoning successes and failures, and proposes typed deltas $ΔK=(ΔS,ΔU,ΔT)$ over instructions, user preferences, and tools. Deltas are version-controlled, evaluated with a sliding window of 1-5 star ratings, auto-repaired on first failure, and rolled back on repeated failure. When an edit budget crosses a threshold, the agent compiles a rating-weighted synthetic set and distills matured instruction-space gains into parameters, converting prompt-space improvements into weight-space without downtime. ILWS makes explicit the low-rank shaping induced by context in transformer blocks, preserves governance, and removes per-call retrieval. In enterprise support it increased throughput 2.4-5.0x and cut audited hallucinations by about 80% versus a frozen baseline. In an Adobe Commerce Cloud proof of concept \"L0 Support\", it achieved 4-5x more tickets per hour and about 80% lower time per ticket, with autonomous instruction updates and optional tool synthesis. Because ILWS operates at the instruction layer until controlled distillation, it generalizes to dynamic domains (legal, medical, engineering) requiring adaptive reasoning, tool creation, and low-latency deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 1 figure, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:28:03Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:28:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:22:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Víctor","familyName":"Mayoral-Vilches","name":"Mayoral-Vilches, Víctor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Per Mannermaa","familyName":"Rynning","name":"Rynning, Per Mannermaa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cybersecurity AI: Hacking the AI Hackers via Prompt Injection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T14:32:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:38:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T20:20:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:44:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We demonstrate how AI-powered cybersecurity tools can be turned against themselves through prompt injection attacks. Prompt injection is reminiscent of cross-site scripting (XSS): malicious text is hidden within seemingly trusted content, and when the system processes it, that text is transformed into unintended instructions. When AI agents designed to find and exploit vulnerabilities interact with malicious web servers, carefully crafted reponses can hijack their execution flow, potentially granting attackers system access. We present proof-of-concept exploits against the Cybersecurity AI (CAI) framework and its CLI tool, and detail our mitigations against such attacks in a multi-layered defense implementation. Our findings indicate that prompt injection is a recurring and systemic issue in LLM-based architectures, one that will require dedicated work to address, much as the security community has had to do with XSS in traditional web applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:32:54Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:32:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:14:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21433","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.21433","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2508.21433","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Lindenbauer","name":"Lindenbauer, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Slinko","name":"Slinko, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ludwig","familyName":"Felder","name":"Felder, Ludwig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor","familyName":"Bogomolov","name":"Bogomolov, Egor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaroslav","familyName":"Zharov","name":"Zharov, Yaroslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Complexity Trap: Simple Observation Masking Is as Efficient as LLM Summarization for Agent Context Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T09:02:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-01T00:23:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T06:16:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:22:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T15:08:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:48:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based agents solve complex tasks through iterative reasoning, exploration, and tool-use, a process that can result in long, expensive context histories. While state-of-the-art Software Engineering (SE) agents like OpenHands or Cursor use LLM-based summarization to tackle this issue, it is unclear whether the increased complexity offers tangible performance benefits compared to simply omitting older observations. We present a systematic comparison of these approaches within SWE-agent on SWE-bench Verified across five diverse model configurations. Moreover, we show initial evidence of our findings generalizing to the OpenHands agent scaffold. We find that a simple environment observation masking strategy halves cost relative to the raw agent while matching, and sometimes slightly exceeding, the solve rate of LLM summarization. Additionally, we introduce a novel hybrid approach that further reduces costs by 7% and 11% compared to just observation masking or LLM summarization, respectively. Our findings raise concerns regarding the trend towards pure LLM summarization and provide initial evidence of untapped cost reductions by pushing the efficiency-effectiveness frontier. We release code and data for reproducibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v3: DL4C camera-ready version to be presented at the 4th DL4C workshop co-located with NeurIPS '25; added OpenHands generality probe, added hybrid context management strategy"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21433","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-01T01:26:58Z","registered":"2025-09-01T01:26:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:01:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seiji","familyName":"Maekawa","name":"Maekawa, Seiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayate","familyName":"Iso","name":"Iso, Hayate","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Bhutani","name":"Bhutani, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Rarity Blind Spot: A Framework for Evaluating Statistical Reasoning in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T21:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T23:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective decision-making often relies on identifying what makes each candidate distinctive. While existing benchmarks for LLMs emphasize retrieving or summarizing information relevant to a given query, they do not evaluate a model's ability to identify globally distinctive features across a set of documents. We introduce Distinctive Feature Mining (DFM), a new task that challenges models to analyze a small-to-medium collection (10-40 documents) and surface features that are rare in the global context (e.g., appearing in less than 10% of documents). This setting mirrors real-world scenarios such as candidate selection or product differentiation, where statistical reasoning, not retrieval, is key. To enable systematic evaluation of this capability, we present DiFBench, a configurable benchmark creation framework with controllable parameters such as document set size and distinctiveness thresholds. Using DiFBench, we perform a large-scale assessment of distinctive feature mining across ten state-of-the-art LLMs. Our findings reveal a significant performance gap between general-purpose and reasoning-enhanced models. All models, however, substantially degrade as the task complexity and document count increase. We also find that a common failure mode is misidentifying frequent features as distinctive. These insights reveal core limitations in contemporary LLMs' abilities to perform fine-grained, statistical reasoning and rarity detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:27:55Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:27:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:36:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":9,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00245%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21433%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21669%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21302%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00251%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04472%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00190%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21422%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2508.21051)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=10"}}